# Goal Description

The goal is to fix the PDF parser so that it can extract vector-based graphics (such as corporate logos, flowcharts, and infographic icons) which are currently being missed because they are not stored as traditional raster images (like JPEG/PNG) in the PDF. We also want to ensure that once extracted, these vector graphics can be properly processed and recognized as icons by the existing `IconExtractor`.

## Proposed Changes

We will modify the core PDF parser to capture vector graphics by finding drawing paths (lines, shapes, fills), grouping them into logical regions, and taking high-quality page screenshots of those specific regions.

### [Component Name: PDF Parser]

#### [MODIFY] [pdf_parser.py](file:///c:/Users/Bhavya/Desktop/Content%20Extraction/app/services/parser/pdf_parser.py)
- **Add Vector Extraction Logic**: Create a new internal method `_extract_vector_graphics(self, page, page_number, start_sequence)`.
  - Use PyMuPDF's `page.get_drawings()` to extract all vector paths on the page.
  - Implement a clustering algorithm to group overlapping or closely adjacent drawing bounding boxes (`rect`s). This ensures a complex flowchart or an icon made of multiple separate shapes is treated as a single image bounding box.
  - Filter out page-sized bounding boxes (to avoid capturing page borders or background fills).
- **Render to Image**: For each clustered bounding box, use `page.get_pixmap(clip=cluster_rect)` to render just that region of the page into a PNG image.
- **Save and Instantiate**: Save the rendered PNG to the `extracted_images_dir` and instantiate an `ExtractedImage` object. 
- **Integrate into Extraction Pipeline**: Update `_extract_page` to call `_extract_vector_graphics` alongside the existing `_extract_images` (which will still be used to catch standard raster photos).

### [Component Name: Icon Extractor]

*(No code changes required, but logic flows here)*
- By converting vector shapes into standard `ExtractedImage` objects (saved as PNGs), the newly captured vector icons will automatically flow into the existing `IconExtractor` in `icons.py`.
- The `IconExtractor` will check their pixel dimensions (which we rendered) and if they are `<= 128x128`, it will hash them and classify them as `ExtractedIcon` items, preserving their original exact bounding box locations from the PDF.

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]
> **Performance Impact:**
> Rendering page pixmaps (taking screenshots) and clustering vector paths adds a slight computational overhead compared to simply reading text. For a standard PDF, this is very fast, but for a 1000-page PDF heavily laden with complex CAD-style vectors, this phase could take longer.

